<Base
  title="About · Informal Engineer"
  description="What Informal Engineer is, what tested means here, and who's behind it."
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Informal Engineer is a lab that publishes free engineered guides and
    research (homelab, self-hosted AI, the tools around them) and sells the
    two things a guide can't be: <a href="/kits/">curated parts kits</a> and
    <a href="/northstack/">managed hosting</a>. It's run by
    <a href="https://meshrahman.com">Mesh Rahman</a>, a licensed engineer who
    documents the gap between what the specs say and what reality delivers.
  </p>

  <p class="section-label">editorial standards</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <strong>What "tested" means here.</strong> If a guide says tested, I ran
      the procedure on the hardware and versions named in its Tested-on block,
      on the date shown. If part of a procedure comes from documentation
      rather than my bench, the guide says so at that step.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Numbers.</strong> Costs are what I actually paid, in CAD, dated.
      Benchmarks state their conditions. No "blazing fast", a number or
      nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Corrections and changelogs.</strong> Every guide keeps a visible
      changelog. If something breaks in a new version, the fix gets a dated
      entry, not a silent edit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Affiliate disclosure.</strong> Kit links are affiliate links
      where a program exists, disclosed on every page they appear. Nothing on
      a kit list I haven't bought and used myself. No sponsored content, no
      third-party pixels, self-hosted cookieless analytics only.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Abandoned experiments stay published.</strong> Negative results
      are data. The honesty is the brand.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="section-label">in memory of tteck</p>
